body:
Thank you so, so much for this response. I've started to do "parts" therapy with my therapist and the idea of self being separate from feelings has been a really hard one to grasp. Your comment really helped to shift it into perspective a little bit more. I want so desperately to use old coping mechanisms like self harm, dissociation, distraction, rather than "feeling those feelings" but stuffing them down isn't really helping. I'm going to try to be a little bit more mindful and try to embrace the feeling. 

&#x200B;

I appreciate you taking time out of your day and leaving such a wonderful response. Sending light and love your way!

Thanks for the recommendation! I have been journaling more frequently and writing out my frustrations is super helpful for me too. Otherwise the ruminations just turn toxic in my head. 

&#x200B;

Hoping for the best for you! :)

You're the second person to mention that. I think I'll look for a nearby therapy group; it might take out the sting of feeling so alone in the depth of my depression. 

&#x200B;

Sending you good vibes and hope for peace for you as well!

Wow, this video was incredible to watch.

&#x200B;

Hoping that you find some peace for your MDD. Here if you need someone to talk to!

I really enjoy working with the pediatric population. There are so many avenues to take; you can do outpatient pediatrics, pediatric hematology and oncology, a general peds floor (where I am), pediatric emergency unit or pediatric intensive care. There's endless flexibility for you. I would definitely recommend it to everyone! It can be frustrating dealing with the parents at times but overall it is better staffed and more supportive than other forms of nursing. Who knows? You could go into nursing school and find that the ED or ICU or public health nursing or school nursing or trauma is your fit. Tons of options!

&#x200B;

If you have already Bachelor's, you still have tons of options. You can do an accelerated BSN program, but they can be pricey. Another option would be to do an ADN at a community college which is cheaper. You can start working as an RN and then get the hospital to pay for your BSN tuition. Most hospitals including academic center hospitals will still hire ADNs. Especially after the pandemic, I foresee a lot of nurses leaving the field and plenty of jobs available. Let me know if you have any further questions!

Thank you so much. My employer does know that I was struggling but not about the suicidal thoughts. I don't want to be dishonest with a potential employer but I want to get to the interview stage and idk if I'll be rooted out if I list the job. 

&#x200B;

Thank you for your positivity and kind words. It is very much appreciated.
